class GeoSpline2(System.Object):
    """Whether a spline touches another shape in the plane.

    A curve in the plane that is not a chain of pieces but one smooth sweep: a NURBS, a B-spline whose control
    points may pull on it with different strengths, which is what an AutoCAD SPLINE and an IFC B-spline curve
    hold.

    It is given by its degree, its control points, its knots and, if it is rational, its weights. The knots cut
    the parameter into spans, and each span is a polynomial (or a ratio of two) of the degree; the curve is
    clamped, so it starts at its first control point and ends at its last, and the control polygon, the chain
    through the control points, shows where it goes without touching it. The control points of a cubic with the
    corners (0, 0), (100, 150), (200, 150), (300, 0) and the knots 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1 give the Bézier curve
    that rises to 112.5 in the middle, at (150, 112.5), not to the 150 of the polygon. A circle is the rational
    quadratic of nine control points that GeoSpline2.FromCircle builds, every point of it at the radius to
    rounding.

    A spline is read over its domain, from GeoSpline2.DomainStart to GeoSpline2.DomainEnd, the first and last
    knot, which are whatever the knots say: the knots are kept as they were given, so a spline read from a file
    goes back out as it came. Two kinds of parameter are told apart by their names, as the other curves keep
    them: the members named for a parameter, such as GeoSpline2.GetPointAtParameter, take a share of the domain
    from 0 to 1, and the ones named for a knot, such as GeoSpline2.GetPointAtKnot, take a value of the domain
    itself, which is what the knots, and a file, say. Splines made here have the domain [0, 1], where the two are
    the same number. Neither is a length: the members named for a distance go by length.

    It is immutable: every operation gives a new spline. It is a class, as GeoPolylineArc2 is, because it holds
    arrays, and what it hands out of them is read-only.

    How far a spline is from a point, and whether a point lies on it, and how far it is from every other shape in
    the plane.

    Where a spline crosses another shape in the plane.

    The point of a spline nearest a point, where on the spline it lies, and the shortest segment joining the two;
    and the shortest segment from a spline to every other shape in the plane.

    How far a spline is from another spline, whether they touch, where they cross and the shortest segment
    between them."""
