class GeoSpline3(System.Object):
    """Whether a spline touches another shape in space.

    A curve in space that is not a chain of pieces but one smooth sweep: a NURBS, a B-spline whose control points
    may pull on it with different strengths, which is what an AutoCAD SPLINE and an IFC B-spline curve hold. It
    is the counterpart of GeoSpline2 in space, made and read the same way.

    It is given by its degree, its control points, its knots and, if it is rational, its weights. The knots cut
    the parameter into spans, and each span is a polynomial (or a ratio of two) of the degree; the curve is
    clamped, so it starts at its first control point and ends at its last. The control points of a cubic (0, 0,
    0), (100, 150, 60), (200, 150, 60), (300, 0, 0) and the knots 0, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1 give the Bézier curve
    that rises to 112.5 and 45 in the middle, at (150, 112.5, 45). A circle is the rational quadratic of nine
    control points that GeoSpline3.FromCircle builds, every point of it at the radius to rounding.

    A spline is read over its domain, from GeoSpline3.DomainStart to GeoSpline3.DomainEnd, the first and last
    knot, which are whatever the knots say: the knots are kept as they were given, so a spline read from a file
    goes back out as it came. Two kinds of parameter are told apart by their names, as the other curves keep
    them: the members named for a parameter, such as GeoSpline3.GetPointAtParameter, take a share of the domain
    from 0 to 1, and the ones named for a knot, such as GeoSpline3.GetPointAtKnot, take a value of the domain
    itself, which is what the knots, and a file, say. Splines made here have the domain [0, 1], where the two are
    the same number. Neither is a length: the members named for a distance go by length.

    It is immutable: every operation gives a new spline. It is a class, as GeoPolylineArc3 is, because it holds
    arrays, and what it hands out of them is read-only.

    How far a spline is from a point, and whether a point lies on it, and how far it is from every other shape in
    space.

    Where a spline crosses another shape in space.

    The point of a spline nearest a point, where on the spline it lies, and the shortest segment joining the two.

    How far a spline is from another spline in space, whether they touch, where they meet and the shortest
    segment between them."""
